            <ArticleTitle>Off-pump coronary surgery: where do we stand in 2010?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>583-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/HCO.0b013e32833f04b0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE OF REVIEW" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">In this review, we will highlight recent studies comparing off-pump coronary artery bypass surgery with traditional on-pump coronary artery bypass surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RECENT FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Current literature comparing off-pump vs. on-pump coronary artery bypass has failed to demonstrate the superiority of one technique over the other. Small prospective randomized controlled trials continue to show equivalent in-hospital outcomes and continue to raise concerns about vein graft patency and completeness of revascularization. Larger observational analyses are better powered to statistically compare in-hospital outcomes in both low and high-risk patients and in general have shown more favorable in-hospital outcomes and equivalent long-term outcomes with off-pump compared with on-pump coronary artery bypass.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Off-pump coronary artery bypass continues to be a useful technique for coronary revascularization. The benefits of off-pump techniques may be more apparent for patients at high risk for complications associated with cardiopulmonary bypass and aortic manipulation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
